<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="practice-substitution">

  <title>Integration by Substitution</title>

  <exercise xml:id="practice-substitution-1">
    <title>Substitution</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Evaluate each integral using an appropriate substitution.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate
          <m>\int 4x^3(4-x^4)^{1/3}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m>u=4-x^4</m>, so
          <m>du=-4x^3\,dx</m>. Then
        </p>

        <me>
          \int 4x^3(4-x^4)^{1/3}\,dx
          =
          -\int u^{1/3}\,du
          =
          -\frac{3}{4}u^{4/3}+C.
        </me>

        <p>
          Therefore,
        </p>

        <me>
          -\frac{3}{4}(4-x^4)^{4/3}+C.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate
          <m>\int_0^{1/2}5\sin(1-2x)\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m>u=1-2x</m>, so <m>du=-2\,dx</m>.
          The limits become <m>u=1</m> and <m>u=0</m>.
        </p>

        <me>
          \int_0^{1/2}5\sin(1-2x)\,dx
          =
          -\frac{5}{2}\int_1^0\sin(u)\,du
          =
          \frac{5}{2}\left(1-\cos(1)\right).
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </exercise>

</section>